  "notes": "すりむく means to scrape or graze the skin, typically from falling down or rubbing against a rough surface.\n\nWRITING:\nCan be written as {擦|す}り{剥|む}く, though usually written in kana.\n\nFORMS:\n{擦|す}る (to rub) + {剥|む}く (to peel) — literally 'to rub off skin.'\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: {他動詞|たどうし} (transitive)\n- Pattern: [body part]をすりむく\n\nASPECT (ている):\nすりむいている describes the resulting state: the skin is scraped/grazed.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {膝|ひざ}をすりむく (scrape one's knee)\n- {肘|ひじ}をすりむく (scrape one's elbow)\n- {手|て}をすりむく (scrape one's hand)\n- {転|ころ}んですりむく (fall and scrape oneself)\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- すり{傷|きず} (scrape, graze — the resulting wound)\n- {擦|す}りむける (intransitive: skin comes off from friction)",
